<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/10/21/apple-iphone-duo-hero-bigjpglarge-2x-1792595146631-6af85fcdcc.jpg" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p2">iPhone Duo reviews are out today, and while I was hoping that this year I’d get to review this hybrid device under embargo, it hasn’t happened (I hope it will, <em>someday!</em>). I’ve spent the past few hours reading, annotating, and watching as many Duo reviews as I could, and I’ve compiled the most interesting bits and common themes in the roundup below.</p>
<p id="p3">The broad strokes are consistent across all the reviews: the Duo’s hinge and the matte inner display are terrific; the lack of Face ID and a telephoto camera are serious trade-offs; and most third-party apps aren’t ready yet. But there’s plenty of nuance in the details, and even some disagreement (about the speakers, of all things).</p>
<p id="p4">Let’s dive in.</p>
<p id="p5"><!--more--></p>
<h3 id="design-and-the-hinge">Design and the Hinge</h3>
<p id="p6">There’s near-unanimous praise for the Duo’s hinge. As <a href="https://petapixel.com/2026/10/21/iphone-duo-review-a-compromise-but-not-compromised/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Jaron Schneider wrote at PetaPixel</a>:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote7"><p>
  This is an intricate, complicated design and the result is an opening and closing experience that is remarkably satisfying. The resistance you feel changes with the angle, and the hinge features a custom alloy that is nitride-treated to preserve it over time. Basically, the Duo’s excellent opening and closing experience is meant to last.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p8"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0Rxd1ehkWBQ&amp;t=153s" rel="noopener noreferrer">Brian Tong</a> called it “the best hinge on a foldable that I’ve experienced because it flows, it feels smooth.” <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxDTWuL9GxY&amp;t=913s" rel="noopener noreferrer">Joanna Stern</a>, meanwhile, had a machine fold her Duo over 32,000 times while she slept:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote9"><p>
  The result, the hinge was actually a little bit stiffer. But in everyday use, I really couldn’t feel a difference. So, after 32,000 folds, it felt pretty much the same as day one, which is pretty darn impressive.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p10">As for durability, <a href="https://gizmodo.com/iphone-duo-review-2000827502" rel="noopener noreferrer">Raymond Wong at Gizmodo</a> ran an unplanned test of his own:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote11"><p>
  I had an unfortunate mishap where the unfolded iPhone Duo slipped out of my hands and fell down a few steps that also happened to be wet from rain. While not an intentional test for durability or the device’s IP68 water and dust resistance rating, the fact that the iPhone Duo survived with no major damage (only a few minor scuffs to the metal frame) and both screens had no scratches or cracks really shows how tough it is.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p12">Opinions on the weight are more mixed. <a href="https://9to5mac.com/2026/10/21/iphone-duo-review/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Chance Miller at 9to5Mac</a> found the Duo easier to hold than its weight would suggest, though not easier to open:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote13"><p>
  But one key difference is weight distribution. Despite being slightly heavier, I find the iPhone Duo significantly more comfortable to hold than the iPhone 18 Pro Max.</p>
<p>  Unfolding the iPhone Duo is very much a two-handed operation. The hinge has enough resistance, and the two halves fit together securely enough that you really need both hands to open it comfortably.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p14"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ESABTN4VtrA&amp;t=324s" rel="noopener noreferrer">Marques Brownlee</a> was more direct: “after you use this thing for like a week or two, you would be happy if it was 20% lighter.”</p>
<h3 id="the-nano-texture-display-and-the-crease">The Nano-Texture Display and the Crease</h3>
<p id="p15">For most reviewers, the matte inner display is the star of the show. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=P5_GBTMAbFI&amp;t=191s" rel="noopener noreferrer">Michael Fisher</a> said that “the inner screen is a masterpiece” and that “this paper-like nano-texture is a revelation that also camouflages the crease beautifully.” <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ESABTN4VtrA&amp;t=522s" rel="noopener noreferrer">Marques</a> agreed:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote16"><p>
  Now, the inside screen is the part of the Duo that does not feel first generation at all. They did an incredible job with it. I love it.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p17"><a href="https://x.com/mweinbach/status/2112879156723581299" rel="noopener noreferrer">Max Weinbach</a> explained what’s going on under the hood:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote18"><p>
  On iPhone Duo, the nano-texture coating is etched into the polymer cover using maskless laser lithography that essentially writes a micro-lens over each pixel individually. Yes, each pixel.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p19">As for the crease, I think <a href="https://9to5mac.com/2026/10/21/iphone-duo-review/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Chance put it best</a>:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote20"><p>
  Here’s the best way I can think of explaining it: anyone who says the iPhone Duo’s crease is invisible is misleading you. Even Apple doesn’t say that. It talks about the work it’s done to reduce the crease’s <em>visibility</em>, but nowhere does it claim the crease is invisible. At the same time, anyone who says the iPhone Duo’s crease is visible enough to be a problem is similarly misleading you.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p21"><a href="https://www.theverge.com/tech/1015028/iphone-duo-review" rel="noopener noreferrer">Nilay Patel and Allison Johnson at The Verge</a> pointed out that you can still feel it:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote22"><p>
  The matte display is indeed there to help hide the crease, which Apple keeps calling “perceptually flat” because it’s more fun to invent science-y marketing phrases than just saying “you mostly don’t see it.” But it’s true: You mostly can’t see the display crease, although you can definitely feel it.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p23"><a href="https://petapixel.com/2026/10/21/iphone-duo-review-a-compromise-but-not-compromised/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Jaron</a>, for one, is into it:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote24"><p>
  Another point that I’ve seen talked about is how it will feel to run your thumbs or fingers across that hinge as you’re using the Duo. “You’ll feel it,” I’ve seen said. Yeah, you do. And you know what? I <em>like</em> it.</p>
<p>  When I’m scrolling Reddit, I kind of get excited for the moment that I’ll drag my thumb down across that hinge. The only way I can describe it is as a subtle, soothing bump. It feels nice!
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p25">Not everyone is sold on matte, though. <a href="https://www.tomsguide.com/phones/iphones/apple-iphone-duo-review" rel="noopener noreferrer">Mark Spoonauer at Tom’s Guide</a> missed the punchiness of a glossy OLED:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote26"><p>
  But while the iPhone Duo’s main display gets plenty bright, it doesn’t offer the perfect blacks or vibrant hues I normally associate with OLED.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p27">And <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxDTWuL9GxY&amp;t=275s" rel="noopener noreferrer">Joanna</a> had a more practical complaint:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote28"><p>
  But what bothered me more were the fingerprints that seep into the screen and that were much harder to clean off than on a glossy screen. Apple, you could have included the $9 polishing cloth with the $2,000 phone.
</p></blockquote>
<h3 id="the-small-outer-display">The Small Outer Display</h3>
<p id="p29">The 5.4-inch cover display is probably the most debated design choice. From <a href="https://www.theverge.com/tech/1015028/iphone-duo-review" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Verge</a>:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote30"><p>
  Apple says you’re getting 90 percent of the screen area of an iPhone 18 Pro, but that’s pretty small for today’s apps. It’s like having an iPhone 5 in a world of iPhone Pro Maxes.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p31"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0Rxd1ehkWBQ&amp;t=1838s" rel="noopener noreferrer">Brian</a> compared it to his old iPhone mini, saying that “it feels really small on the cover screen. Too small.” <a href="https://9to5mac.com/2026/10/21/iphone-duo-review/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Chance</a> ran into the limits of the outer display in Safari:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote32"><p>
  But the awkwardness of the iPhone’s 5.4-inch outer display shows up in Safari. The web simply isn’t optimized for this form factor.</p>
<p>  It’s easy to find yourself on a Safari webpage where you can’t see anything but a cookie popup or newsletter banner. Granted, you can’t really blame Apple for that. It’s clear the modern web isn’t optimized for such a small, squat form factor.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p33"><a href="https://x.com/Davidimel/status/2112888102964310171" rel="noopener noreferrer">David Imel</a>, on the other hand, sees the small screen as a feature:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote34"><p>
  The Duo feels like the most task-oriented iPhone ever. That’s because the outer display is small, so when I’m doing anything substantial, I need to commit to opening it and using the inner display.
</p></blockquote>
<h3 id="righties-lefties-and-volume-buttons">Righties, Lefties, and Volume Buttons</h3>
<p id="p35">With the dock and app controls on the right side of the screen, the Duo is, in <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ESABTN4VtrA&amp;t=414s" rel="noopener noreferrer">Marques’ words</a>, “a very right-handed phone.” <a href="https://www.cnet.com/tech/mobile/iphone-duo-review/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Abrar Al-Heeti at CNET</a> had a message for left-handed users:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote36"><p>
  But my condolences to all the lefties, as you can’t shift controls to the other side of the screen.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p37">The volume buttons, which are now on the top edge, take some getting used to as well. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxDTWuL9GxY&amp;t=637s" rel="noopener noreferrer">Joanna</a>:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote38"><p>
  And that means when you’re on a phone call, I almost never hit the right button because I have to tap up here and I can’t feel which is up or which is down.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p39"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0Rxd1ehkWBQ&amp;t=1389s" rel="noopener noreferrer">Brian</a> went as far as saying:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote40"><p>
  In some cases, in some ways, I actually found the Z Fold 8 more natural to use than the iPhone because they have the dock on the bottom.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p41"><a href="https://www.theverge.com/tech/1015028/iphone-duo-review" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Verge</a> summed it up nicely:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote42"><p>
  A lot of using the Duo is just figuring out how to use an iPhone again, which hasn’t happened in a long time.
</p></blockquote>
<h3 id="third-party-apps-arent-ready-yet">Third-Party Apps Aren’t Ready (Yet)</h3>
<p id="p43">The biggest problem at launch appears to be third-party apps for the Duo. <a href="https://www.theverge.com/tech/1015028/iphone-duo-review" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Verge</a> explained the three levels of app support, including what they call the “messy middle”:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote44"><p>
  The second category is what you might call the messy middle: apps that have been updated for iOS 27. They get <em>some</em> automatic layout fixes so they’ll automatically expand to fill the inner screen in landscape orientation and don’t look quite as silly as standard iPhone apps. But you can tell they haven’t <em>actually</em> been designed for these displays, and those rough edges are super obvious.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p45"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxDTWuL9GxY&amp;t=697s" rel="noopener noreferrer">Joanna</a> was less diplomatic:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote46"><p>
  So, how do I say this nicely to some of the third party apps I’ve been testing. You’re drunk. They’re sloppy. They’re inconsistent. And they just sometimes refuse to behave.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p47">(She also found <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxDTWuL9GxY&amp;t=754s" rel="noopener noreferrer">a calendar app for ants</a>.) <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ESABTN4VtrA&amp;t=969s" rel="noopener noreferrer">Marques</a> had a similar experience:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote48"><p>
  But the general experience has been flipping between a whole bunch of not optimized apps that look pretty terrible on the Duo right now.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p49">Things are moving fast, though. <a href="https://x.com/mweinbach/status/2112879156723581299" rel="noopener noreferrer">According to Max</a>:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote50"><p>
  The reason I want to bring this up is because over the ~6 days I’ve been using my iPhone Duo, apps went from not at all optimized (except for Perplexity and Claude), to nearly half of them being updated.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p51">And <a href="https://www.wired.com/review/apple-iphone-duo/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Julian Chokkattu at Wired</a> made a fair point:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote52"><p>
  But it is a little maddening that some app developers, who for years have never seemed to care about optimizing their app for Android folding phones, are so quick to embrace Apple’s fold.
</p></blockquote>
<h3 id="the-fun-apps">The Fun Apps</h3>
<p id="p53">When an app is properly optimized, <a href="https://www.wired.com/review/apple-iphone-duo/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Julian says</a>, “the experience is <em>chef’s kiss</em>.” Several reviewers also fell for apps built specifically for the Duo’s form factor. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=P5_GBTMAbFI&amp;t=753s" rel="noopener noreferrer">Michael</a> said that “Tiny Laptops is quite possibly my favorite iOS app of the past 5 years,” and <a href="https://gizmodo.com/iphone-duo-review-2000827502" rel="noopener noreferrer">Raymond</a> is optimistic about what’s coming:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote54"><p>
  Every day I open X and see imaginative app designs for the iPhone Duo, like a Nintendo DS emulator for playing retro games, and it feels like we could be on the verge of app renaissance for the iPhone platform. That’s exciting in a way that it hasn’t been in more than a decade.
</p></blockquote>
<h3 id="multitasking-and-the-ipad-question">Multitasking and the iPad Question</h3>
<p id="p55">The Duo is the first iPhone that can run two apps side by side, but the implementation is more limited than on iPad. <a href="https://9to5mac.com/2026/10/21/iphone-duo-review/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Chance</a>:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote56"><p>
  I wish there were more iPadOS elements sprinkled throughout the iPhone Duo’s software. It’s great having Split View, but I wish there was an iPadOS-like way to drag and drop apps into that Split View interface. <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/125309" rel="noopener noreferrer">Slide Over</a> also seems like a piece of low-hanging fruit.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p57"><a href="https://www.wired.com/review/apple-iphone-duo/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Julian</a>:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote58"><p>
  If you have two apps split-screen on the Duo, Apple won’t let you resize one app to be slightly bigger than the other like you can on Android. Here, a 50-50 split is always enforced.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p59">Portrait orientation is even more limited, according to <a href="https://www.theverge.com/tech/1015028/iphone-duo-review" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Verge</a>:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote60"><p>
  If you want to have two apps open when you’re holding the Duo in portrait mode, you’re basically out of luck — unless what you want is to watch a video on the top while scrolling an app on the bottom.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p61"><a href="https://www.wired.com/review/apple-iphone-duo/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Julian</a> also tried working with a keyboard and trackpad, which only worked after enabling AssistiveTouch:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote62"><p>
  If the Duo is the iPhone that lets you do more, Apple should natively enable cursor support just like it does on iPads.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p63"><a href="https://x.com/mweinbach/status/2112879156723581299" rel="noopener noreferrer">Max</a>, interestingly, prefers the constraints:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote64"><p>
  Something I’ve also found myself enjoying a lot about the iPhone Duo is actually the fact that multi-tasking is a bit more limiting.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p65"><a href="https://x.com/mweinbach/status/2112882939545387348" rel="noopener noreferrer">Max also confirmed on X</a> that Apple Pencil support is coming with iOS 27.2</p>
<h3 id="poses-standby-and-watching-stuff">Poses, StandBy, and Watching Stuff</h3>
<p id="p66">As <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxDTWuL9GxY&amp;t=293s" rel="noopener noreferrer">Joanna</a> put it, “Apple’s basically turned the iPhone into a yoga class.” <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ESABTN4VtrA&amp;t=1347s" rel="noopener noreferrer">Marques</a> thinks the half-open poses are where the Duo stands out:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote67"><p>
  But the other thing it’s actually really good at better than a lot of other foldables is the halfway open state because this hinge is so sturdy in the middle that it’s probably the best foldable at just being partially open.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p68"><a href="https://gizmodo.com/iphone-duo-review-2000827502" rel="noopener noreferrer">Raymond</a> noted that “the iPhone Duo’s StandBy mode works even when it’s not plugged in,” and <a href="https://www.tomsguide.com/phones/iphones/apple-iphone-duo-review" rel="noopener noreferrer">Mark</a> found a good use for the laptop pose:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote69"><p>
  For example, when watching “Man of Steel” for the hundredth time on the Apple TV app, I could put the Duo in tabletop mode like a laptop, and it showed the video up top and the playback controls down below.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p70">For watching and reading, <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QkAbWwFoH7c&amp;t=458s" rel="noopener noreferrer">iJustine</a> said that “the Duo as a content viewing machine is just that. It is a machine,” and <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dxF1r2BMqjU&amp;t=794s" rel="noopener noreferrer">Tyler Stalman</a> thinks that, for some people, “if you use an iPad a lot, this might be able to replace both.”</p>
<h3 id="touch-id">Touch ID</h3>
<p id="p71">The lack of Face ID is the most common complaint across all reviews. <a href="https://9to5mac.com/2026/10/21/iphone-duo-review/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Chance wrote</a>:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote72"><p>
  I think switching from Face ID to Touch ID has been the hardest thing to get used to on iPhone Duo. I’ve become so accustomed to just looking at my iPhone to unlock it. Having to deliberately reach for the side button feels like a step backward, even though Touch ID works quickly and reliably.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p73"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ESABTN4VtrA&amp;t=1192s" rel="noopener noreferrer">Marques</a>:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote74"><p>
  I really think Face ID is going to be the number one thing people miss coming from other iPhones. Not the zoom cameras, not the thinner and lighter form factors. I think it’s Face ID.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p75"><a href="https://petapixel.com/2026/10/21/iphone-duo-review-a-compromise-but-not-compromised/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Jaron’s advice</a>:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote76"><p>
  You’re going to want to register all 10 of your fingers, too, because you never hold this thing the same way twice.</p>
<p>  I have found that Touch ID is slower than Face ID for unlocking the phone. It got to the point where I was so grateful that I have an Apple Watch because I could set it to automatically unlock it when it sensed I was wearing the watch. Unlock with Watch is also, somehow, faster than using Touch ID.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p77"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0Rxd1ehkWBQ&amp;t=1260s" rel="noopener noreferrer">Brian</a> came to the same conclusion: “you will want to wear an Apple Watch.” <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxDTWuL9GxY&amp;t=648s" rel="noopener noreferrer">Joanna</a> was more emotional about it: “Face ID. I cannot tell you how deeply I miss you. Please come back.” <a href="https://www.theverge.com/tech/1015028/iphone-duo-review" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Verge</a>, however, got over it quickly:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote78"><p>
  But a week is long enough to get used to Touch ID life again, and it’s mostly painless once you remember you’re supposed to be using it.
</p></blockquote>
<h3 id="cameras">Cameras</h3>
<p id="p79">The Duo’s dual cameras are a step down from the iPhone 18 Pro, and reviewers are split on how much that matters. <a href="https://petapixel.com/2026/10/21/iphone-duo-review-a-compromise-but-not-compromised/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Jaron</a>:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote80"><p>
  The Duo cameras are all middling in quality. That doesn’t mean you can’t get great photos with the iPhone Duo; it just means that there are way better cameras available even in Apple’s own product line.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p81"><a href="https://www.theverge.com/tech/1015028/iphone-duo-review" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Verge</a>:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote82"><p>
  The Duo relies on heavier sharpening to make up for its weaker hardware, so fine details look a little crunchier than they do on the 18 Pro.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p83"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dxF1r2BMqjU&amp;t=175s" rel="noopener noreferrer">Tyler</a>, who shoots professionally with his iPhone, was more forgiving:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote84"><p>
  Looking at the image quality of the Pro, it does use a bigger, better sensor. There’s no getting around that. It’s a bit nicer than the Duo, but most people are not going to spot the difference.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p85"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=P5_GBTMAbFI&amp;t=475s" rel="noopener noreferrer">Michael</a> put the Duo up against the Galaxy Z Fold 8 and said, “I don’t think it’s a stretch to call this the best camera on a foldable in the US.” <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ESABTN4VtrA&amp;t=781s" rel="noopener noreferrer">Marques</a> described the Duo as “the productivity entertainment iPhone that happens to have good enough cameras,” but he also called the under-display camera on the inner screen “<a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ESABTN4VtrA&amp;t=571s" rel="noopener noreferrer">trash</a>” for photos. And <a href="https://x.com/Davidimel/status/2112888102964310171" rel="noopener noreferrer">David</a> is disappointed by the missing pro features:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote86"><p>
  There’s also no ProRAW or ProRes, which is disappointing for a $2,000 device.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p87">The good news, as <a href="https://www.theverge.com/tech/1015028/iphone-duo-review" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Verge noted</a>, is that “<em>every</em> camera on this thing is a selfie camera.”</p>
<h3 id="battery-life">Battery Life</h3>
<p id="p88">Most reviewers came away impressed with battery life. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=P5_GBTMAbFI&amp;t=838s" rel="noopener noreferrer">Michael</a>:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote89"><p>
  Battery life in particular is just outstanding. I’m talking 10 to 20% left at bedtime after a 17-hour day with hours of gaming or camera sessions, sometimes both.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p90"><a href="https://www.wired.com/review/apple-iphone-duo/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Julian said</a>, “With average use, I’ve ended most days with around 40 percent left by bedtime,” and <a href="https://x.com/Davidimel/status/2112888102964310171" rel="noopener noreferrer">David wrote</a>:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote91"><p>
  I’m always shocked when I still have 90% battery remaining after a few hours off the charger.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p92">Not everyone agrees, though. <a href="https://petapixel.com/2026/10/21/iphone-duo-review-a-compromise-but-not-compromised/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Jaron</a> called it “just barely good enough,” and <a href="https://www.theverge.com/tech/1015028/iphone-duo-review" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Verge</a> concluded that the Duo “doesn’t seem ready to last a full day of heavy use.”</p>
<h3 id="heat">Heat</h3>
<p id="p93">Several reviewers noticed the Duo getting warm under load. <a href="https://petapixel.com/2026/10/21/iphone-duo-review-a-compromise-but-not-compromised/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Jaron</a>:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote94"><p>
  The Duo can also get warm if you work it. Playing games, editing photos, or doing anything that stressed the processor resulted in pretty noticeable warmth on the right side of the phone when unfolded. It will then gradually spread that heat out across the spine and over to the other side of the phone if you keep using it.</p>
<p>  The iPhone 18 Pro is really, really good at heat dissipation to the point that it never really feels hot in a single spot. Rather, the whole phone feels warmer — I intentionally am not using the word hot here. The iPhone Duo still feels hot at a single point when the processor is really chugging.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p95"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxDTWuL9GxY&amp;t=444s" rel="noopener noreferrer">Joanna</a>:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote96"><p>
  Apple says the warmth is actually a sign of the new thermal design doing its job, moving heat away from the chip to keep performance up. But to me, it just feels like the phone is really warm.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p97"><a href="https://www.theverge.com/tech/1015028/iphone-duo-review" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Verge</a> saw it affect performance with camera features:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote98"><p>
  Add in processor-intensive camera features like Smart Take, which automatically hits the shutter button, and the phone gets hot enough to start noticeably lagging after a while.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p99"><a href="https://9to5mac.com/2026/10/21/iphone-duo-review/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Chance</a>, on the other hand, said, “I do think the iPhone Duo runs a bit hotter than the iPhone 18 Pro, but not in a way that impacts performance.”</p>
<h3 id="speakers">Speakers</h3>
<p id="p100">And now for the most surprising disagreement of the bunch. <a href="https://9to5mac.com/2026/10/21/iphone-duo-review/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Chance</a> wasn’t impressed:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote101"><p>
  I do think the iPhone Duo’s speakers leave a little bit to be desired. I wasn’t overly impressed with the speaker quality, and I think there’s a notable disparity compared with the iPhone 18 Pro. The iPhone Duo’s speakers sound thinner and quieter to my ears.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p102"><a href="https://petapixel.com/2026/10/21/iphone-duo-review-a-compromise-but-not-compromised/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Jaron</a>, however, loved them:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote103"><p>
  Luckily, Apple knocked this one out of the park: they are loud, have surprisingly good low-end rendition, and excellent clarity. Big thumbs up.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p104"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0Rxd1ehkWBQ&amp;t=466s" rel="noopener noreferrer">Brian</a> called the Duo “the best sounding foldable that I’ve experienced to date,” and <a href="https://www.tomsguide.com/phones/iphones/apple-iphone-duo-review" rel="noopener noreferrer">Mark’s take</a> was simple: “Surprisingly good!”</p>
<h3 id="wrapping-up">Wrapping Up</h3>
<p id="p105"><a href="https://www.theverge.com/tech/1015028/iphone-duo-review" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Verge’s conclusion</a> captures the general mood well:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote106"><p>
  It’s incomplete as a product, but complete enough as an idea that you can see where things are going.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p107"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ESABTN4VtrA&amp;t=1656s" rel="noopener noreferrer">Marques</a> thinks some trade-offs are here to stay:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote108"><p>
  Apple can’t completely engineer their way around the compromises of this form factor, like the slightly worse battery and the slightly worse speakers and the slightly worse cameras. Like, that’s just physics.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p109"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxDTWuL9GxY&amp;t=960s" rel="noopener noreferrer">Joanna</a> is more hopeful:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote110"><p>
  The good news is most of my other frustrations can be fixed with software updates and developers figuring out how to make great apps for two very different screen sizes.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p111">I hope to be able to get my hands on an iPhone Duo soon. Once that happens, expect a <em>lot</em> of coverage on MacStories for the next few weeks. Stay tuned.</p>
